inl task_KINO_PARALLEL_CACHE () = Task ("KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE", "Kino stress notebook: N Spiral cells over one shared prefix run concurrently; measure per-cell compile time vs cold, require each cell's output identical to a single-cell run (catches cross-request state leaks like FRONTIER fix 57); if caching holds, migrate single-module samples to notebooks; 2026-10-08: Kino CompilerDaemon serializes every compile (GenServer handle_call -> one single-flight compiler process, warm after the first request), so concurrent cells queue instead of running in parallel; the experiment needs a compiler that serves parallel requests from one warm process, i.e. HOPAC-WARM-SERVER (fix 57: per-request join point state), then a daemon pool or async dispatch; 2026-10-08 17:0x unblocked differently: single-flight is warm-safe, so concurrency needs no hopac: a pool of K single-flight daemons (client-side, one port each); memory measured (tmp/kino-mem/warm-growth.ps1, one warm --batch process, 190 example compiles): ~530 MB after 189 compiles (~2.5 MB retained per compile), a single runaway sample (runaway_inline_match_recursion, FOR-D) spiked to 2 GB; the 5.5 GB Kino server seen this morning was a heavy compile's working set, not accumulated leaks; K=2-3 fits this 16 GB machine for normal cells; 17:4x implemented the pool: CompilerClient spreads compiles over SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILERS single-flight daemons (ports 13905..; default 1 = the old path exactly; least-busy pick through atomic ETS counters; ensure/retire/locks/logs per port; shutdown retires all); Kino tests 53/53; stress (tmp/kino-mem/stress.exs: 6 cells, fresh source copies, every daemon warmed first): pool 1 seq 16.9 s / par 9.4 s, pool 3 seq 17.5 s / par 6.7 s (1.4x over pool 1, 2.6x over sequential); outputs identical seq vs par and across pools; caches are per daemon (a repeated source is a cache hit only on the daemon that compiled it); cold daemon ~7 s first compile", Feature, P2, D5, 60u32, Active, "HOPAC-WARM-SERVER", "kino,compiler,cache,parallel", "current", "current")
inl task_WORKSPACE_PRIMITIVES_LIB () = Task ("WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB", "One granular Spiral library of workspace primitives (fs, processes with supervision/timeout/elapsed, git read, tables TSV/CSV/JSON/psd1, time, env, hash) returning enriched results; eoie verbs become thin wrappers over it; the same library is what converted repo scripts call (46% of 6,804 pwsh calls are glue this replaces); 2026-10-08 first slice: eoie src/workspace_primitives (source-only Spiral package, one module per concern: outcome validation_result, numbers, bytes, clock unix_ms/ms_since, console stdout/stderr receipts, files checked atomic replace + size receipts); the 7 crates written today (agile add/edit/list/census/init, text-replace, fs-remove) open it; 4 of them regenerated byte-identical, 3 changed only the generic failure wording", Epic, P1, D7, 10u32, Active, "", "eoie,spiral,library", "current", "current")
inl task_PS1_TO_SPIRAL () = Task ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", "After HOPAC-SPEED reaches state of the art (user): rewrite each repo's .ps1 scripts (build, test, init, workflow) as Spiral programs compiled to Rust, living in their own repos, compiled and run through the spiral CLI, using WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB; no shelling out to pwsh or eoie", Epic, P3, D7, 0u32, Blocked, "", "scripts,spiral,repos", "current", "current")
inl task_HOPAC_WARM_SERVER () = Task ("HOPAC-WARM-SERVER", "Make the hopac core stable as a long-lived warm server (fix 57: per-request join point state keyed per request) so harness and Kino stop paying ~10 s warm-up per process; prerequisite for Kino SOTA and for HOPAC-SPEED numbers; scoping 2026-10-08 (FRONTIER.md fix 57): warm --batch already compiles a suite 12x faster (186 s vs 2,295 s) with 1,054/1,089 rows unchanged; the ~35-100 diverging rows come from JP work items whose hard-deadline watchdog (left by a build the host abandoned) sets the process-wide terminal-failure latch inside a later build; point resets (latch, invalidation count, sequential flag, per-request watchdog ordinal) are done, tagging work items per request did not help; the remaining design is one per-BuildFile session object owning JP work items, cells, caches, deadlines and the latch, created per request and dropped after it; single-flight is already warm in the Kino daemon; 2026-10-08 12:3x DONE for the harness: host defers a failure from a process that already served a build (withholds the result, exit 4; the runner resumes that job first in a fresh process); -WarmRecycle is hopac default; same DLL -Suite all: 782 s vs 1,834 s fresh (2.35x), 2,014/2,014 rows identical (status, residual hash, verdict); 605 deferrals, 106 warm-only failures caught; remaining for Kino/fully warm (~12x): the per-BuildFile session object; 16:3x deployed hopac (CodegenC fixes + readable names) on the default warm-recycle path, -Suite all -Parallel 2: 2,024 jobs in 851 s, every oracle row in the parity class (no missing/REGRESSED), 53 parity-residual-differs = the 37 known + 14 rows of the three lib/spiral samples (variable numbering inside lib backend_switch code differs between the cores, not names)", Epic, P1, D7, 60u32, Active, "", "compiler,hopac,kino", "current", "current")
inl task_KINO_SOTA () = Task ("KINO-SOTA", "Kino to state of the art: warm compiler server, concurrent cells over a shared prefix with correct memoization (KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE), fast incremental re-run, rich outputs", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "", "kino", "current", "current")
